---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import data from "../../data/processed/ecosystem.json";
---

<Layout title="Methodology">
  <div class="page-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">Method / Reproduce & challenge</div>
    <h1>Make the boundaries visible.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Public evidence can help us ask better questions. It cannot describe the
      whole community.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Questions & sampling</h2>
    <p>
      How does a newcomer find a starting point? Where are contribution and
      support channels visible? What can issue and pull-request workflows tell
      us about public engagement, and where could education reduce friction?
    </p>
    <p>
      We purposefully selected GL JS as the core browser SDK, then Draw and
      Geocoder as developer-facing plugins with visible activity in the
      collected window. These are three repositories, not a random sample of
      Mapbox’s ecosystem. Search JS was checked but its candidate GitHub URL
      returned 404.
    </p>
    <p>
      The 90-day window balances a bounded collection budget against recent
      activity. It is not seasonally representative. Exact window:{" "}
      <code>{data.window.start}</code> through <code>{data.window.end}</code>,
      inclusive. Collection began at <code>{data.collectedAt}</code>. API calls
      occur sequentially, not as an atomic snapshot.
    </p>
    <h2>CHAOSS references & operational definitions</h2>
    <p>
      Definitions were checked against the{" "}
      <a href="https://www.chaoss.community/kb-metrics-and-metrics-models/">
        current CHAOSS metrics catalog
      </a>{" "}
      on November 4, 2026. This is CHAOSS-inspired reconnaissance, not a claim
      of full conformance.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.chaoss.community/kb/metric-issues-new/">
          Issues New
        </a>
        : count non-PR issue creation events in the window; all states included.
        GitHub’s issues endpoint also returns PRs, which we separate.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.chaoss.community/kb/metric-change-requests/">
          Change Requests
        </a>
        : count PRs created in the window. This is volume, not acceptance,
        quality, or unique contributors.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.chaoss.community/kb/metric-change-requests-duration/">
          Change Requests Duration
        </a>
        : CHAOSS uses accepted/merged requests. Our{" "}
        <strong>PR closure proxy</strong> instead measures hours from creation
        to closure for created-window PRs closed by window end, including
        unmerged closures. We do not retrieve merge status. GL JS’s contribution
        guide describes an internal import and Copybara sync process in which
        accepted public PRs can appear closed rather than merged; GitHub merge
        flags alone would also need contextual interpretation. Report median
        with closed denominator; open PRs are right-censored. This favors faster
        completed work and is not review duration.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.chaoss.community/kb/metric-time-to-first-response/">
          Time to First Response
        </a>
        : our <strong>sampled issue-comment proxy</strong> uses the newest five
        issues per repository created in the window, or all if fewer. Earliest
        comment by someone other than the author, excluding accounts typed Bot
        by GitHub, up to window end. Self and bot comments are excluded.
        Reactions, issue events, reviews, discussions, and other channels are
        absent. Undeclared bot accounts can remain. Median uses responded items
        only; unanswered count is always shown. The small newest-first sample
        has recency and censoring bias.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Contribution discoverability check</strong>: a qualitative
        inventory of named CONTRIBUTING, SUPPORT, LICENSE, and CODE_OF_CONDUCT
        files, supplemented by linked README and guide review. This is a local
        proxy, not a named CHAOSS metric. File presence does not establish
        usability, acceptance policy, or newcomer success.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <h2>Acquisition & reproducibility</h2>
    <p>
      <code>npm run research:collect</code> retrieves public metadata, recursive
      default-branch trees, issues updated since window start, and comments for
      the bounded issue sample. Creation timestamps select the actual cohort.
      All paginated issue and comment endpoints follow GitHub’s next-page link
      indication. A 30-page guard fails rather than emitting partial metrics.
      Tree truncation is disclosed.
    </p>
    <pre>npm ci
npm run research:collect
npm test
npm run check
npm run lint
npm run build</pre>
    <p>
      Use Node 22.12+ (Node 24 tested). Set <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> in the
      process environment for higher rate limits; Node scripts do not
      automatically load .env. Never expose it through PUBLIC_ variables.
      Optional <code>RESEARCH_END</code> pins the cohort end in ISO format.
      Requerying an old window can differ because GitHub records change or
      disappear; use the committed snapshot for exact numerical reproduction.
    </p>
    <p>
      The collector retries transient server and 429 failures with bounded
      delays, honors Retry-After up to 30 seconds, and stops with
      remaining-rate/reset diagnostics on other failures. It does not silently
      substitute zeroes. Failed runs leave the previous snapshot intact. GitHub
      authentication and API limits can restrict collection.
    </p>
    <h2>Data provenance & privacy</h2>
    <p>
      <code>data/raw/github.json</code> is normalized evidence, not a full API
      response dump. It retains repository metadata, item numbers, source links,
      creation and closure times, and sampled comment timestamps with self/bot
      flags. Names, emails, profile data, and comment text are omitted.{" "}
      <code>data/processed/ecosystem.json</code> contains presentation
      aggregates. Editorial conclusions live in page source, separate from
      generated data. Both JSON files are published for inspection.
    </p>
    <p>
      Conference locations are curated historical venue points, verified against
      organizer pages linked in each card. Coordinates were manually
      approximated at venue level; they are not survey-grade. This dataset is
      for testing map interactions, not for estimating developer population,
      current attendance, or Mapbox community reach.
    </p>
    <h2>What we cannot conclude</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        No inference about employee performance, internal strategy,
        organizational dysfunction, or private support quality.
      </li>
      <li>
        No identity resolution, contributor employer attribution, demographic
        inference, or maintainer ranking.
      </li>
      <li>
        Automation remains in activity totals. One participant may create many
        items.
      </li>
      <li>
        Deleted, transferred, inaccessible, or moderated activity may be
        missing. GitHub is a changing data source.
      </li>
      <li>
        No firsthand interviews or usability studies were conducted.
        Contribution guides were not tested by outside contributors.
      </li>
      <li>
        One snapshot cannot establish a trend or intervention effect. Geographic
        examples are unrelated to the GitHub sample.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Human review & publication</h2>
    <p>
      Before sharing as the author’s portfolio: verify licenses and contribution
      requirements, review every interpretation, complete firsthand field notes,
      supply a restricted public Mapbox token, and record a successful
      production map render. AI-assisted analysis remains provisional until that
      review.
    </p>
    <p>
      Source layout: <code>src/pages</code> presentation,{" "}
      <code>scripts/research</code> acquisition and metric calculations,{" "}
      <code>data</code> snapshots, and <code>docs/methodology</code> provenance.
      GitHub Pages deployment and exact setup instructions are in README.md.
      GitHub repository publication is pending until an authenticated remote is
      configured.
    </p>
    <h2>Primary technical references</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://docs.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/guides/get-started/">
          GL JS getting started
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://docs.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/example/data-driven-circle-colors/">
          Data-driven circle styling
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://docs.mapbox.com/help/dive-deeper/access-tokens/">
          Token scopes and URL restrictions
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/rest/issues/issues#list-repository-issues">
          GitHub issues API
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
